Squashed deliberately. The three changes below were sent as separate patches and
none of them landed, because scripts/apply-inbox.sh tests after EVERY patch and two
of its own guards — "no patch is committed anywhere" and "the inbox is empty" —
are red for as long as any other patch is still waiting in the inbox. A multi-patch
run could therefore never succeed, and a single-patch run failed too because a
stray patch file sat tracked at the repository root. One patch, one run, no chicken
and egg. The narrative is kept here rather than lost.

--- 1. The capacity read had to be able to leave the browser tab (was 0021)

The free qualifier produced a real engineering read and then lost all of it when
the tab closed. Qualifications now mint a token (18 random bytes, base64url) and
resolve at /q/<token>: noindex, force-dynamic, shareable with the colleague who
actually owns the budget. persist() no longer swallows a write failure — it throws,
and the API returns share: null, because handing out a link to a row that was never
written is worse than handing out no link. benchmark() compares a hall against a
minimum of eight others, selecting only binding_constraint and racks_as_found so a
benchmark can never leak a hall.

Requires supabase/migrations/0008_qualification_token.sql. Until that migration is
run the share link does not resolve.

--- 2. One domain, one company, one price list (was 0022)

The site was selling two businesses. /infrastructure quoted megawatts and real
estate we do not own and never will — the capital rule is explicit and it is the
whole shape of the venture: no owned cooling inventory, no owned energy assets, no
data-center hardware, physical deployment customer-funded. That page, lib/catalog.ts,
ConfigStudio and SingleLineDiagram are deleted.

lib/site.ts is now the single domain registry: SITE_URL and siteUrl(path), nothing
else may hardcode a domain. lib/products.ts is the only file in which a euro figure
may appear — the chat system prompt was still quoting a band that had moved, and
"deposit against EUR 22k-45k" had the 22 in the engine and the 45 typed into a React
component, which made the upper half of every quoted range unsourceable. Engagement
gains price_eur_max; products gain opensBandCents; tests/test_one_company.py fails
the build on a euro figure outside the catalogue, on a second domain, and on a page
that sells plant we do not have.

--- 3. The guard was right, the script's reading of it was wrong

scripts/apply-inbox.sh reverted a correct patch twice, on live main, for a failure
it did not cause. A patch uploaded through the GitHub web interface lands at the
repository ROOT; the root-hygiene test correctly went red; the script then applied
an unrelated patch, ran the whole suite, saw red, and rolled it back. It could not
tell "this patch broke the repository" from "the repository was already broken".

It now sweeps *.patch off the root into inbox/ itself — committing the move when the
file is tracked — and records which tests were already failing BEFORE it applies
anything, rolling back only on NEW failures. Pre-existing failures are printed by
name at the start and again at the end so they still get fixed. A patch that repairs
a baseline failure shrinks the baseline, so a later patch in the same run cannot
smuggle it back in. tests/test_patch_intake.py drives the script against a throwaway
repository and asserts all of it, for the reason written at the top of
tests/test_deploy_script.py: a shell script with no test is how a bug survives the
patch that claims to fix it.

--- 4. A cancelled Hall Watch went on being delivered

Billing was never the problem — checkout runs in Stripe subscription mode and
renews by itself. The reverse did not work. customer.subscription.deleted and
invoice.payment_failed resolved the subscription id against api_accounts ONLY, never
against watches, so a cancelled watch's status never left "active", dueWatches()
filters on exactly that, and the weekly cron kept generating and sending quarterly
change notes to somebody who had stopped paying. Free work, on a schedule, with
nothing in the system that would ever notice.

Both tables are now resolved on all three lifecycle events. A failed payment pauses
rather than cancels, because Stripe retries and a card that fails on Tuesday and
clears on Thursday should not cost the client a quarter.
tests/test_stack_consistency.py asserts the join for every recurring product.

--- 5. HANDOFF.md

The brief a new agent or engineer starts from, linked from the README. The mandate
and the capital rule verbatim; the commercial case, including why the cost library
and the calibration ledger are the two assets that cannot be bought or scraped; the
architecture of engine and site; every test suite and the specific failure it exists
to prevent; the patch history 0001-0023 and what each one bought commercially; and
the complete open list — operational, legal, product, engineering, distribution — in
the order the founder's own sequence says to work it.

388 tests pass. tsc --noEmit clean. next build clean: /q/[token] present,
/infrastructure gone.

